          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The climactic or darkest hour of crisis, especially an unexpected moment when God intervenes in judgment or deliverance and people must be spiritually ready.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"Matthew 25:1–13, especially 25:6: the bridegroom arrives at midnight, commonly applied to Christ’s unexpected return and the need for readiness.\",\"Exodus 11:4–6; 12:29–31: at midnight God judges Egypt’s firstborn and initiates Israel’s deliverance.\",\"Acts 16:25–26: Paul and Silas pray and sing at midnight before the earthquake opens the prison.\",\"Mark 13:32–37, especially 13:35: midnight is one possible watch in which the returning master may arrive, underscoring vigilance and an unknown time.\",\"Job 34:20: midnight is associated with sudden judgment and death.\"] ,\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The Second Coming of Christ specifically, with the “midnight cry” representing the announcement immediately preceding his arrival.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 25:6\",\"Mark 13:35\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Spiritual or moral darkness—the lowest point of suffering, danger, or apparent hopelessness.\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 12:29–31\",\"Acts 16:25–26\",\"Job 34:20\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A turning point from darkness toward deliverance, renewal, or a new beginning.\",\"basis\":[\"Exodus 12:29–31\",\"Acts 16:25–26\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A special hour of prayer, praise, or devotion.\",\"basis\":[\"Psalm 119:62\",\"Acts 16:25\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"A prophetic chronological marker identifying a precise historical date, era, or end-times timetable.\",\"basis\":[\"Matthew 25:6, interpreted through particular prophetic systems rather than an explicit biblical definition\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"There is no single settled symbolic meaning for every biblical occurrence of “midnight”; many uses are simply temporal. The strongest recurring interpretive cluster joins darkness or crisis, sudden divine action, and the need for watchfulness. In explicitly eschatological Christian teaching, the unexpected coming of Christ is often foregrounded.\"}"
        }
